---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { frontendSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
/* ── Hero ── */
.fe4-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#00b894 0%,#0984e3 60%,#6c5ce7 100%)}

/* ── Animated tree building ── */
.dom-tree-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;position:relative;width:14rem;height:11rem}
.dom-node{position:absolute;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.6rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.3rem 0.6rem;border-radius:0.4rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.15);border:0.0625rem solid rgba(255,255,255,0.3);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);white-space:nowrap;opacity:0;animation:nodeFade 0.6s ease forwards}
.dom-node.n-doc{top:0.25rem;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);animation-delay:0s}
.dom-node.n-html{top:2.5rem;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);animation-delay:0.4s}
.dom-node.n-head{top:5rem;left:1rem;animation-delay:0.8s}
.dom-node.n-body{top:5rem;right:1rem;animation-delay:0.8s}
.dom-node.n-h1{top:8rem;left:0;animation-delay:1.2s}
.dom-node.n-p{top:8rem;right:0;animation-delay:1.2s}
.dom-connector{position:absolute;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.25);opacity:0;animation:nodeFade 0.4s ease forwards}
.dc-1{width:0.0625rem;height:1.5rem;top:1.4rem;left:50%;animation-delay:0.2s}
.dc-2{width:0.0625rem;height:1.5rem;top:3.7rem;left:50%;animation-delay:0.6s}
.dc-3{width:4rem;height:0.0625rem;top:4.95rem;left:2.2rem;animation-delay:0.8s}
.dc-4{width:0.0625rem;height:1.75rem;top:6.25rem;left:3rem;animation-delay:1s}
.dc-5{width:0.0625rem;height:1.75rem;top:6.25rem;right:3rem;animation-delay:1s}
@keyframes nodeFade{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(4px) translateX(-50%)}to{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0) translateX(-50%)}}
.dom-node.n-head,
.dom-node.n-body,
.dom-node.n-h1,
.dom-node.n-p{animation-name:nodeFadeNoX}
@keyframes nodeFadeNoX{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(4px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}

.fe4-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}

/* ── Quiz theming ep4 ── */
.quiz-ep4f .quiz-q-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep4f .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep4f .quiz-opt-letter{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep4f .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep4f .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#e8f8f5;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero fe4-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 4 of 22</div>
  <h1>The DOM —<br>The Browser's Living Tree</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">When your browser receives HTML, it builds something remarkable — a living tree you can change in real time.</p>
  <div class="dom-tree-art">
    <div class="dom-connector dc-1"></div>
    <div class="dom-connector dc-2"></div>
    <div class="dom-connector dc-3"></div>
    <div class="dom-connector dc-4"></div>
    <div class="dom-connector dc-5"></div>
    <div class="dom-node n-doc">document</div>
    <div class="dom-node n-html">&lt;html&gt;</div>
    <div class="dom-node n-head">&lt;head&gt;</div>
    <div class="dom-node n-body">&lt;body&gt;</div>
    <div class="dom-node n-h1">&lt;h1&gt;</div>
    <div class="dom-node n-p">&lt;p&gt;</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe4-num">01</span>
  <h2>HTML Is Just a Text File</h2>
  <p>When you write an HTML file, you are writing a <span class="highlight">plain text document</span>. Open any .html file in a text editor and you see exactly that — characters, tags, and words. There is no magic in it. It is just text.</p>
  <p>When you send that text file to a browser, the browser reads it top to bottom, character by character. But a browser cannot directly manipulate plain text — it cannot find elements, change them, move them, or animate them. You cannot say "find the third paragraph in this string of characters and change its color."</p>
  <p>So the browser does something crucial: it parses the HTML text and builds a <span class="highlight-orange">data structure in memory</span>. A structure that represents every element in the page as an object that code can work with. That structure is the <span class="highlight">DOM</span>.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe4-num">02</span>
  <h2>What DOM Stands For</h2>
  <p>DOM stands for <span class="highlight">Document Object Model</span>. Each word is meaningful:</p>

  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.5rem">
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Document</strong> — the HTML page. The DOM represents the whole page.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Object</strong> — each HTML element (h1, p, div, button) becomes a JavaScript object with properties and methods.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Model</strong> — these objects are arranged in a tree structure that mirrors the nesting in your HTML.
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">So the DOM is a <span class="highlight">tree of JavaScript objects</span>, built by the browser from your HTML file, living in memory, representing every element on the page.</p>
</section>

<!-- tree diagram -->
<div class="reveal" style="max-width:43.75rem;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1.5rem">
  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">HTML file (text):</span>
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;html&gt;</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">&lt;head&gt;</span>
    <span class="code-keyword">&lt;title&gt;</span>My Page<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/title&gt;</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">&lt;/head&gt;</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">&lt;body&gt;</span>
    <span class="code-keyword">&lt;h1 id=</span><span class="code-string">"title"</span><span class="code-keyword">&gt;</span>Hello<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/h1&gt;</span>
    <span class="code-keyword">&lt;p&gt;</span>Welcome.<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">&lt;/body&gt;</span>
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/html&gt;</span>

<span class="code-comment">DOM tree (memory):</span>
<span class="code-string">document</span>
  <span class="code-arrow">└─</span> <span class="code-keyword">html</span>
       <span class="code-arrow">├─</span> <span class="code-keyword">head</span>
       <span class="code-arrow">│    └─</span> <span class="code-keyword">title</span>  <span class="code-comment">"My Page"</span>
       <span class="code-arrow">└─</span> <span class="code-keyword">body</span>
            <span class="code-arrow">├─</span> <span class="code-keyword">h1#title</span>  <span class="code-comment">"Hello"</span>
            <span class="code-arrow">└─</span> <span class="code-keyword">p</span>  <span class="code-comment">"Welcome."</span></pre>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: live -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe4-num">03</span>
  <h2>The DOM Is Live</h2>
  <p>Here is the most important property of the DOM: it is <span class="highlight">live</span>. When you change a node in the DOM tree, the browser immediately re-renders what the user sees. The screen updates in real time.</p>
  <p>This is the bridge between code and what users see:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// Find a node by ID and change its text</span>
document.getElementById(<span class="code-string">'title'</span>).innerText = <span class="code-string">'New Title'</span>;
<span class="code-comment">// → Screen updates immediately. No server round trip.</span>

<span class="code-comment">// Change a style</span>
document.getElementById(<span class="code-string">'banner'</span>).style.display = <span class="code-string">'none'</span>;
<span class="code-comment">// → Element disappears instantly.</span>

<span class="code-comment">// Create a new element and add it to the tree</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> p = document.createElement(<span class="code-string">'p'</span>);
p.innerText = <span class="code-string">'New paragraph'</span>;
document.body.appendChild(p);
<span class="code-comment">// → New paragraph appears at bottom of page.</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>JavaScript accesses the DOM through a global object called <code style="font-size:0.875rem">document</code>. This object is the root of the tree and provides methods to find, create, modify, and remove nodes.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: DOM vs HTML -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe4-num">04</span>
  <h2>The DOM Is NOT Your HTML File</h2>
  <p>This is a subtle but important distinction that confuses many beginners. The DOM and your HTML file are <span class="highlight-orange">two different things</span>.</p>

  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.5rem">
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Your HTML file</strong> is static. It is the text on disk. JavaScript adding 10 new elements to the page does <em>not</em> change your HTML file. The file is unchanged.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">The DOM</strong> is dynamic. It lives in the browser's memory. It starts as a copy of your HTML file, but JavaScript can change it freely without touching any file.
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">When you right-click a page and choose <strong>Inspect</strong> or <strong>View Source</strong>:</p>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem">
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">Inspect / DevTools Elements tab</strong> — shows the <em>current DOM</em>. Reflects all JavaScript changes.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">View Page Source (Ctrl+U)</strong> — shows the <em>original HTML file</em> that was sent from the server. No JavaScript changes.
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">React and Vue build their entire UI by creating DOM nodes with JavaScript. Your HTML file for a React app might be almost empty — just a single <code style="font-size:0.875rem">&lt;div id="root"&gt;</code>. The DOM is filled in entirely by JavaScript after the page loads.</p>
</section>

<div class="analogy reveal">
  <p>Think of your HTML file as a <strong>blueprint</strong> and the DOM as the <strong>actual house</strong>. The blueprint is what was designed. The house is what you can live in and rearrange. Interior decorators (JavaScript) rearrange the house all the time — but the blueprint doesn't change.</p>
</div>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">The DOM</strong> is a tree of JavaScript objects the browser builds from your HTML, living in memory.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">It is live</strong> — changing a DOM node instantly updates what the user sees on screen.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">DOM ≠ HTML file</strong> — the HTML file is static on disk. The DOM is the browser's live, in-memory representation.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">JavaScript accesses</strong> the DOM through the global <code style="font-size:0.875rem">document</code> object.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep4f">
  <span class="section-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What does DOM stand for?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Dynamic Object Mapping</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Direct Object Method</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Document Object Model</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Data Output Module</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Document Object Model</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Document = the HTML page. Object = each element becomes a JavaScript object. Model = arranged in a tree structure. Together: a tree of JavaScript objects representing the page.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">When you change a node in the DOM, what happens?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The HTML file on disk is updated</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>A request is sent to the server</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>The browser immediately re-renders what the user sees</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>The page must be refreshed to show the change</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) The browser immediately re-renders what the user sees</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">The DOM is live. Any change to a DOM node — changing text, hiding an element, adding a new node — is reflected on screen immediately without any server round trip or page reload.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">When you open DevTools and inspect a page, what are you seeing?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The original HTML file exactly as served from the server</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>The current state of the DOM, including all JavaScript changes</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>The JavaScript source code</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>The server-side template before HTML is generated</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) The current state of the DOM, including all JavaScript changes</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">DevTools' Elements tab shows the live DOM. If JavaScript has added or removed elements, those changes are visible here. "View Source" (Ctrl+U) shows the original HTML file from the server — before any JavaScript ran.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 04</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">How does JavaScript access the DOM?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Through the window.html global object</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>By reading the .html file directly</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Through the global document object</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Through HTTP requests to a local server</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Through the global document object</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">The browser provides a global object called document which is the root of the DOM tree. Methods like document.getElementById(), document.createElement(), and document.querySelector() are how JavaScript navigates and modifies the tree.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#e17055,#0984e3)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 5?</h2>
  <p>The DOM was a great idea — but in the early days, Netscape and Microsoft implemented it completely differently. The browser wars nearly destroyed the web. Here is how that fight played out.</p>
  <a href="part5.html" class="btn btn-white">Continue to Episode 5 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How the Frontend Works — Part 4: The DOM | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={frontendSeries} episodeNum={4} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
